<header class="section-header">
  <h1 class="section-title">Privacy Policy</h1>
</header>

<section class="prose-container">
  <p><strong>Helix Equine Ltd</strong><br />
  Last updated: 21 October 2026</p>

  <p>At Helix Equine Ltd, we respect your privacy and are committed to protecting your personal information. This Privacy Policy explains how we collect, use, store and share personal data when you use our veterinary services, visit our website or contact us.</p>

  <p>We handle personal information in accordance with applicable UK data protection legislation, including the UK General Data Protection Regulation (UK GDPR), the Data Protection Act 2018 and the Privacy and Electronic Communications Regulations 2003 (PECR), as amended.</p>

  <h2>1. Who we are</h2>
  <p>Helix Equine Ltd ("Helix Equine", "we", "us" or "our") is an equine veterinary practice.</p>
  
  <p>
    Helix Equine Ltd<br />
    Callaly Long House<br />
    Callaly<br />
    Alnwick<br />
    Northumberland<br />
    NE66 4TA<br />
    Email: <a href="mailto:info@helixequine.co.uk">info@helixequine.co.uk</a><br />
    Website: <a href="https://www.helixequine.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">www.helixequine.co.uk</a>
  </p>

  <p>Helix Equine Ltd is the data controller responsible for the personal information described in this policy. This means we determine how and why your personal data is processed.</p>

  <p>If you have any questions about this policy or how we handle your information, please contact us using the details above.</p>

  <h2>2. Information we collect</h2>
  <p>We collect and process personal information necessary to provide veterinary services, manage our business and communicate with our clients.</p>

  <p><strong>Information you provide to us</strong></p>
  <p>When you register as a client, book an appointment, request veterinary advice, purchase goods or services, or communicate with us, we may collect:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your name, postal address, email address and telephone numbers.</li>
    <li>Details of the horses you own, keep or are responsible for, including identification, passport and microchip information.</li>
    <li>Your horse's veterinary history, examination findings, diagnostic results, medication, treatments and ongoing care.</li>
    <li>Billing details, transaction records, payment history and outstanding balances.</li>
    <li>Insurance provider and policy information, where relevant.</li>
    <li>Records of appointments, enquiries and communications with us.</li>
    <li>Any other information you choose to provide that is relevant to our services.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Information about a horse is not, by itself, personal data under UK data protection law. However, records linking a horse to an identifiable owner, keeper or client may contain personal data and will be handled accordingly.</p>

  <p><strong>Information from other sources</strong></p>
  <p>Where appropriate, we may receive relevant information from other veterinary practices, referral hospitals, laboratories, insurers, yard managers, trainers, previous owners or keepers, and relevant regulatory or government bodies.</p>
  <p>We only collect information from these sources where there is an appropriate reason and lawful basis to do so.</p>

  <p><strong>Information collected through our website</strong></p>
  <p>When you visit our website, limited technical information, such as your IP address, browser information and basic server access records, may be processed by our website hosting or security providers.</p>
  <p>We do not currently use non-essential analytics, advertising or behavioural tracking cookies.</p>
  <p>Please see Section 12 for further information about cookies and website functionality.</p>

  <h2>3. How we use your information</h2>
  <p>We use your personal information for the following purposes:</p>
  
  <p><strong>Providing veterinary care:</strong> To register clients and horses, arrange consultations and visits, provide examinations, diagnoses and treatments, prescribe and supply veterinary medicines, maintain clinical records and support continuity of care.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Appointments and communication:</strong> To contact you about appointments, veterinary advice, treatment plans, results, follow-up care, vaccination reminders and other matters concerning your horse.</p>

  <p><strong>Accounts and payments:</strong> To issue invoices, process payments, maintain financial records, manage insurance claims and recover outstanding debts where necessary.</p>

  <p><strong>Referrals and professional services:</strong> To communicate with referral practices, specialists, laboratories, insurers and other relevant professionals involved in your horse's care.</p>

  <p><strong>Certification and regulatory requirements:</strong> To complete veterinary certificates, export documentation, identification records and other regulatory paperwork, and comply with applicable legal and professional obligations.</p>

  <p><strong>Business administration:</strong> To respond to enquiries, resolve complaints, maintain our records, manage our practice and improve the services we provide.</p>

  <p><strong>Website operation and security:</strong> To maintain the functionality and security of our website, protect against misuse and investigate technical problems.</p>

  <p><strong>News and marketing:</strong> To provide information about our services, practice news or relevant offers where you have consented or where applicable marketing rules otherwise permit us to do so.</p>

  <p>We will not use personal information for purposes incompatible with those explained in this policy unless the law permits or requires us to do so.</p>

  <h2>4. Our lawful basis for processing your information</h2>
  <p>Under UK data protection law, we must have a lawful basis for processing personal information. Depending on the circumstances, we rely on:</p>

  <p><strong>Performance of a contract:</strong> Where processing is necessary to provide veterinary services, fulfil an agreement with you or take steps at your request before entering into a contract.</p>

  <p><strong>Legal obligation:</strong> Where processing is necessary to comply with applicable laws and regulatory requirements, including financial record-keeping, veterinary medicines legislation and statutory duties.</p>

  <p><strong>Legitimate interests:</strong> Where processing is reasonably necessary to operate our veterinary practice, provided those interests are not overridden by your rights and freedoms. This may include maintaining appropriate clinical and business records, managing payments, protecting our systems, responding to enquiries, arranging routine care reminders and improving our services.</p>

  <p><strong>Consent:</strong> Where we require your permission for a particular activity, such as certain direct marketing communications. You may withdraw your consent at any time. Withdrawal will not affect processing lawfully carried out before consent was withdrawn.</p>

  <p>If you do not provide information necessary for us to register you, treat your horse or meet our legal obligations, we may be unable to provide some or all of our services.</p>

  <h2>5. Sharing your information</h2>
  <p>We respect client confidentiality and do not sell personal information to third parties.</p>
  <p>We may share relevant information where necessary, appropriate and lawful with:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Other veterinary professionals:</strong> including referral hospitals, specialists and veterinary practices involved in your horse's care.</li>
    <li><strong>Laboratories and diagnostic providers:</strong> to arrange tests, obtain results and support clinical decision-making.</li>
    <li><strong>Insurance providers:</strong> where you have requested or authorised our involvement in an insurance claim, or where another lawful basis applies.</li>
    <li><strong>Professional service providers:</strong> including our practice management software provider, payment processors, banking services, accountants, IT support providers and website hosting providers.</li>
    <li><strong>Professional advisers and insurers:</strong> including legal advisers, professional indemnity insurers and debt recovery providers where necessary.</li>
    <li><strong>Regulatory and government bodies:</strong> including the Royal College of Veterinary Surgeons (RCVS), Veterinary Medicines Directorate (VMD), Animal and Plant Health Agency (APHA), HMRC and other relevant authorities where disclosure is required or permitted by law.</li>
    <li><strong>Business successors:</strong> where our business is sold, transferred or restructured, subject to appropriate confidentiality and data protection arrangements.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Where third-party providers process personal information on our behalf, we require appropriate contractual and security arrangements.</p>
  <p>We will seek any consent or authorisation required before disclosing confidential clinical information, unless disclosure is otherwise justified or permitted by law, including where animal welfare or the public interest requires it.</p>
  <p>We only share information reasonably necessary for the relevant purpose.</p>

  <h2>6. International transfers</h2>
  <p>Some third-party service providers may store or process personal information outside the United Kingdom.</p>
  <p>Where personal data is transferred internationally, we take steps to ensure appropriate legal protections are in place. These may include UK adequacy regulations or approved contractual safeguards recognised under UK data protection law, together with any additional protections required.</p>
  <p>You can contact us for further information about international transfers and applicable safeguards.</p>

  <h2>7. How long we keep your information</h2>
  <p>We retain personal information only for as long as reasonably necessary for the purposes for which it was collected, including compliance with legal, professional, accounting and insurance requirements.</p>

  <p>Our retention periods include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clinical and client records:</strong> normally retained for six years following the last relevant veterinary service, subject to any longer retention required for ongoing care, legal obligations, insurance matters or potential claims.</li>
    <li><strong>Financial and accounting records:</strong> generally retained for at least six years, or longer where required by applicable tax or accounting legislation.</li>
    <li><strong>Veterinary medicines and certification records:</strong> retained in accordance with applicable statutory requirements and professional guidance.</li>
    <li><strong>Enquiries and general correspondence:</strong> retained for as long as reasonably necessary to deal with the matter and maintain appropriate records.</li>
    <li><strong>Marketing information:</strong> retained while you remain subscribed or until it is no longer required. We may retain limited information to respect unsubscribe requests.</li>
    <li><strong>Website technical information:</strong> retained for periods appropriate to website operation, security and troubleshooting, in accordance with our hosting and IT arrangements.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Where information is relevant to an ongoing clinical case, complaint, dispute or legal claim, we may retain it for longer where justified.</p>
  <p>When information is no longer required, we will securely delete, destroy or anonymise it.</p>

  <h2>8. How we protect your information</h2>
  <p>We take the security and confidentiality of personal information seriously.</p>
  <p>We use appropriate technical and organisational measures designed to protect personal data against unauthorised access, accidental loss, misuse, alteration and disclosure. These measures include appropriate access restrictions, secure record management, protection of electronic systems and confidentiality obligations for those handling client information.</p>
  <p>We review our data handling and security arrangements as appropriate.</p>
  <p>Although we take reasonable steps to protect information, no electronic transmission or storage system can be guaranteed to be completely secure.</p>
  <p>We ask clients to notify us if their contact information or other relevant details change so that our records remain accurate and up to date.</p>

  <h2>9. Your data protection rights</h2>
  <p>Under UK data protection law, you have certain rights in relation to your personal information. Depending on the circumstances, these include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Right of access:</strong> to request a copy of the personal data we hold about you.</li>
    <li><strong>Right to rectification:</strong> to ask us to correct inaccurate or incomplete personal information.</li>
    <li><strong>Right to erasure:</strong> to request deletion of your personal information in certain circumstances.</li>
    <li><strong>Right to restrict processing:</strong> to ask us to limit how your information is used in certain situations.</li>
    <li><strong>Right to object:</strong> to object to certain processing, including processing based on legitimate interests.</li>
    <li><strong>Right to data portability:</strong> to receive certain personal information in a structured, commonly used and machine-readable format where this right applies.</li>
    <li><strong>Right to withdraw consent:</strong> to withdraw consent where processing is based on consent.</li>
    <li><strong>Rights relating to automated decision-making:</strong> where applicable, to exercise protections provided by law concerning qualifying solely automated decisions.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These rights are not absolute. In some circumstances, we may be required or permitted to retain information, including where necessary to comply with professional or legal obligations.</p>
  <p>To exercise your rights, please contact <a href="mailto:info@helixequine.co.uk">info@helixequine.co.uk</a>.</p>
  <p>We may ask you to provide reasonable information to verify your identity before responding.</p>
  <p>We will normally respond within one month, although this period may be extended by up to a further two months where legally permitted for complex requests or multiple requests. We will explain any applicable extension.</p>
  <p>Requests are normally handled free of charge. In limited circumstances permitted by law, we may charge a reasonable fee or refuse a request.</p>

  <p><strong>Complaints</strong></p>
  <p>If you have concerns about how we process your personal information, please contact us so that we can investigate and respond. You can submit a data protection complaint by emailing <a href="mailto:info@helixequine.co.uk">info@helixequine.co.uk</a> or writing to our postal address.</p>
  <p>We will acknowledge and investigate complaints in accordance with applicable legal requirements, keep you informed where appropriate and provide an outcome without undue delay.</p>
  <p>You also have the right to complain to the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO), the UK's independent data protection regulator:</p>
  <p>
    Website: <a href="https://www.ico.org.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">www.ico.org.uk</a><br />
    Telephone: <a href="tel:03031231113">0303 123 1113</a>
  </p>

  <h2>10. Marketing communications</h2>
  <p>We may occasionally contact clients with practice news, information about our veterinary services or other relevant updates.</p>
  <p>We will only send marketing emails or text messages where you have given appropriate consent or the law permits us to contact existing customers about our own similar products or services, provided all applicable requirements are met.</p>
  <p>You may unsubscribe from marketing communications at any time using the opt-out instructions provided or by contacting <a href="mailto:info@helixequine.co.uk">info@helixequine.co.uk</a>.</p>
  <p>We will respect your marketing preferences.</p>
  <p>Communications relating directly to veterinary care, appointments, treatment, vaccination reminders, invoices and other necessary service matters are generally service communications rather than direct marketing.</p>

  <h2>11. Telephone calls, emails and text messages</h2>
  <p>We may contact you by telephone, email, text message or post where reasonably necessary to provide veterinary care and manage your account. This may include appointment confirmations, results, treatment updates, medication information, vaccination reminders and payment enquiries.</p>
  <p>If you have a preferred method of contact, please let us know and we will endeavour to accommodate it where practical.</p>
  <p>We ask clients to use appropriate discretion when sending confidential or sensitive information by email or other electronic means.</p>

  <h2>12. Cookies and website functionality</h2>
  <p>Our website may use strictly necessary cookies or similar technologies to support its essential operation, functionality and security.</p>
  <p>We do not currently use cookies for advertising, behavioural tracking or non-essential website analytics.</p>
  <p>Strictly necessary cookies do not require your consent under UK law. Where such technologies are used, we provide information about their purpose.</p>
  <p>You can manage or delete cookies through your browser settings, although restricting certain cookies may affect how the website functions.</p>
  <p>We will review this information if the technologies used on our website change.</p>
  <p>For a list of essential cookies used on this site please visit our <a href="/cookies">cookie policy page.</a></p>

  <h2>13. Children's information and sensitive personal data</h2>
  <p>Our veterinary services are generally arranged through adult clients, owners or individuals responsible for a horse.</p>
  <p>We do not intentionally collect personal information from children unless necessary and appropriate in connection with our services.</p>
  <p>Where a child or young person is involved in the ownership or care of a horse, we may ask a parent or guardian to assist with registration, consent and communications as appropriate.</p>
  <p>We do not ordinarily require special category personal data, such as information about your health, religious beliefs or ethnicity.</p>
  <p>If such information is provided and is relevant to our services, we will only process it where a valid legal basis and any additional conditions required by data protection law apply. Otherwise, we will avoid retaining unnecessary sensitive information.</p>

  <h2>14. Links to other websites</h2>
  <p>Our website may contain links to third-party websites or online services. We are not responsible for the privacy practices or content of external websites.</p>
  <p>We encourage you to read their privacy policies before providing personal information.</p>

  <h2>15. Changes to this Privacy Policy</h2>
  <p>We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time to reflect changes in our services, business practices or applicable legislation.</p>
  <p>The current version will be available on our website.</p>
  <p>Where changes are significant, we will take appropriate steps to bring them to your attention.</p>
  <p>Last updated: 21 October 2026</p>
</section>


</BaseLayout>